<h2>The litigation in brief</h2>
<p>The parties married on 14 February 2013 and set up their matrimonial home in Bangalore. The wife later filed DVC No. 64 of 2019 before the Judicial Magistrate, Additional Mahila Court, Krishnagiri — the district where she resided — seeking residence and protection orders. On 21 February 2023 the Magistrate granted both: a residence order in respect of the Bangalore house and a protection order, while dismissing her maintenance prayer. The husband&rsquo;s side appealed; the Additional Sessions Judge, Krishnagiri dismissed the appeals (CA Nos. 33 of 2023 and 13 of 2023) on 5 July 2024. Two civil revision petitions under Article 227 followed, pressed on a single point: the Bangalore property lies outside the Krishnagiri Magistrate&rsquo;s territorial limits, so the residence order was said to be without jurisdiction.</p>

<h2>Section 27, read whole</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Section 27(1) — who has jurisdiction.</strong> The Magistrate within whose local limits the aggrieved person resides (permanently or temporarily), carries on business or is employed; or where the respondent does so; or where the cause of action arose.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Section 27(2) — how far the order reaches.</strong> &ldquo;Any order made under this Act shall be enforceable throughout India.&rdquo; The reach of the relief is national, whatever the district of the court that grants it.</div>
</div>
<p>The petitioners relied on a Madhya Pradesh High Court decision, <em>Dr. Hitesh v. Dr. Smt. Pritibala</em> (2023), for the proposition that a residence order cannot travel beyond the issuing court&rsquo;s limits. The wife answered with the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decision in <em>Shyamlal Devda v. Parimala</em> ((2020) 3 SCC 14) and with the text of Section 27(2). Justice S. Sounthar decided the point on the statute itself. Since the wife resided within the Krishnagiri Magistrate&rsquo;s limits, the complaint was properly entertained: the court noted that &ldquo;the aggrieved person namely the respondent resides within the territorial limits of Magistrate&rdquo; (para 9). And on the central question, the court held that Section 27(2) supplies the answer: the order is enforceable throughout India, and &ldquo;the place of the residence in respect of which residential order is passed is not relevant&rdquo; (para 10). The conclusion was unqualified: &ldquo;I do not find any jurisdictional error or infirmity in the order passed by the learned Magistrate&rdquo; (para 11). Both revisions were dismissed without costs; the residence and protection orders stand.</p>

<h2>Why the design makes sense</h2>
<p>The Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 is built around the aggrieved woman&rsquo;s convenience, not the respondent&rsquo;s or the property&rsquo;s. A woman driven out of her matrimonial home frequently returns to her parental town — often in another district or State. If the residence remedy could be granted only by the court where the house stands, the statute&rsquo;s most important relief would be available only at the one place the woman may find hardest to litigate in. Section 27(1) therefore lets her sue where she is, and Section 27(2) ensures that what she wins there binds the respondent wherever the shared household may be.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>File where she resides.</strong> Temporary residence suffices under Section 27(1); the provision says &ldquo;permanently or temporarily resides&rdquo;.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Relief follows the household.</strong> The residence order under Section 19 attaches to the shared household, wherever situated.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Enforcement is national.</strong> Section 27(2) makes every order under the Act enforceable throughout India.</div>
</div>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>For the aggrieved person: plead the jurisdictional facts under Section 27(1) precisely — the address of current residence, with supporting material — because jurisdiction over the person is what carries the order across district lines.</p>
  <p>For respondents: a territorial objection aimed only at the location of the property is now a weak point in Tamil Nadu, and the reasoning — resting on the central statute — will be persuasive elsewhere. Objections are better directed at whether the premises qualify as a shared household at all.</p>
  <p>Residence orders operating on out-of-State property are executed through the machinery of the Act — the Magistrate, Protection Officer and the police of the place concerned — precisely because Section 27(2) commands nationwide enforceability.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article describes the court&rsquo;s reasoning for general information. The decision is of the Madras High Court; in other States it is persuasive, but the statutory text of Section 27 that it applies is the same everywhere in India.</div>
